          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Caustic vs Oxygen Stripper",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/caustic-vs-oxygen-deck-stripper/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the difference between caustic and oxygen deck strippers?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Caustic strippers use sodium hydroxide to break down the binder holding a finish together, so they strip films like solid stain and paint. Oxygen strippers use sodium percarbonate to oxidise and lift pigment sitting in the wood, so they handle penetrating stains well and barely touch films."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will an oxygen stripper remove solid stain?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually not. Solid stain is a pigmented acrylic that forms a genuine film, and percarbonate does not attack the binder holding that film together. You will get a dulled surface with patchy pigment removal and a ghost of the old colour. Solid stain generally needs a caustic product, ideally as a gel."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does caustic stripper damage wood?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It changes it. Sodium hydroxide reacts with tannins and darkens the wood markedly, especially cedar and redwood, and it degrades lignin, so an over-dwelt application leaves a fuzzy surface needing sanding. Used within its window and followed by an acid brightener, the darkening reverses and the damage is minor."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Which deck stripper is safe for plants?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Sodium percarbonate is far more forgiving; it breaks down into washing soda, water and oxygen. It is not entirely harmless but it is in a different category from caustic, which kills plants on contact, etches aluminium and glass, and stains concrete and pavers. On a raised deck over planting, percarbonate is much easier to use responsibly."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I tell which stripper my deck needs?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Look at how the old finish is failing. Peeling and flaking in sheets means a film finish and you need caustic. Fading, greying and wearing thin in traffic paths with no edges to pick at means a penetrating finish and percarbonate will handle it gently. A water test helps: if water soaks straight in, the finish is largely spent."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I need a brightener after using an oxygen stripper?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It is worth doing but far less critical than after caustic. Percarbonate is only mildly alkaline and does not cause tannin darkening, so the brightener acts as a colour-evening finishing step rather than a chemical necessity. After a caustic strip it is mandatory."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I mix or combine the two types?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Do not mix them in a container. You can, however, use them in sequence on a deck with mixed conditions: caustic on the areas where a film survives, then a percarbonate clean over the whole deck to even out the grey and the traffic paths, followed by an acid brightener as the final step."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
  ],
};
