          <a href="#wood-damage" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. What Each Does to Wood</a>
          <a href="#brightener" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Why Caustic Demands Brightener</a>
          <a href="#safety" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Safety and Plants</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The wrong choice here is the most common reason a deck stripping project fails, and it almost always fails in the same direction: a mild product bought for a film finish that needed an aggressive one.
        </p>

        <div id="two-chemistries" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Two Chemistries, Side by Side
          </h2>

          <p>
            Nearly every deck stripper on a consumer shelf is built on one of two active ingredients, and the packaging rarely makes which one obvious. Turn the bottle over and read the ingredients rather than the marketing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sodium hydroxide, also sold as caustic soda or lye, works by breaking down the binder that holds a finish together. It attacks films directly and aggressively, which is why it handles solid stain and paint. Products described as heavy duty, professional strength, or specifically for solid stain and paint are almost always caustic.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sodium percarbonate, usually marketed as an oxygen stripper or oxygenated cleaner, releases hydrogen peroxide in water and works by oxidising and lifting pigment that sits in and lightly on the wood. It is effective on penetrating stains, gentle on wood, and relatively benign around plants. Products described as eco-friendly, plant-safe, or wood-safe are usually percarbonate.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm"></th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Caustic (NaOH)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Oxygen (Percarbonate)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Attacks</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Binder in the film</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Pigment in the surface</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Strips solid stain</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Poorly or not at all</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Strips paint</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, with a gel</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Strips semi-transparent</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, aggressively</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, well</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Darkens wood</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Significantly</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Slightly</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Raises grain</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rarely</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Plant safe</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Largely</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Brightener required</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, mandatory</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Recommended</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Typical dwell</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">15-45 min</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">15-30 min</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The row that matters most is the third. A percarbonate product will not strip paint and usually stalls on solid stain, and no amount of extra dwell or scrubbing changes that, because the chemistry is not attacking the right thing. This single mismatch accounts for most of the one-star reviews on both categories of product.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SHORT VERSION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Film finish means caustic. Penetrating finish means percarbonate.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If the old finish peels, it is a film and you need caustic. If it fades and wears away, it penetrates and percarbonate will handle it more gently and with less cleanup. Decide which you have before you decide which bottle to buy.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="caustic" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Sodium Hydroxide: The Aggressive Option
          </h2>

          <p>
            Caustic strippers are the workhorse for anything that formed a film, and their strengths and costs are both real.
          </p>

          <p>
            What they do well is break through cured acrylic and alkyd films. Solid stain, deck paint and old varnish all yield to caustic where percarbonate does nothing. They also work relatively quickly, in a 15 to 45 minute dwell, and they are inexpensive for the work they do. On a painted deck there is essentially no alternative short of sanding.
          </p>

          <p>
            The first cost is what they do to wood colour. Sodium hydroxide reacts with tannins and lignin in the timber and darkens it markedly, often to a mottled orange-brown that looks alarming. This is normal and largely reversible with an acid brightener, but the reversal is a required step rather than a cosmetic option, which the next-but-one section covers.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second cost is grain raising. Caustic attacks lignin, which is the natural binder between wood fibres, so an over-dwelt application on softwood leaves a fuzzy, furred surface that needs sanding before finishing. This is why the instruction to watch the deck rather than the clock matters: the window between working and over-working is real.
          </p>

          <p>
            The third is handling. Caustic solutions cause chemical burns, and the risk is concentrated exactly when you are kneeling and scraping over a treated surface. They also etch aluminium and glass, stain concrete and pavers, and kill plants they contact. None of this makes them unsuitable for a deck; it makes site protection and protective equipment genuinely non-negotiable rather than advisory.
          </p>

          <p>
            For paint and heavy solid stain specifically, buy caustic in a gel or thickened form. The active ingredient is the same, but a gel clings to vertical surfaces, holds moisture through a long dwell, and can be covered with plastic sheet. On a horizontal deck in summer a thin caustic liquid can dry before it has finished working, which wastes the application entirely.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="oxygen" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Sodium Percarbonate: The Gentle Option
          </h2>

          <p>
            Percarbonate strippers are genuinely good products with a narrower range, and much of their poor reputation comes from being used outside it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Within their range they are excellent. On a weathered semi-transparent or penetrating oil stain, a percarbonate stripper lifts the old pigment, cleans mildew and grey surface fibre at the same time, and leaves the wood close to its natural colour. Because it also acts as a cleaner, it frequently does the strip and the clean in one operation, which a caustic product does not.
          </p>

          <p>
            They are much more forgiving. Over-dwelling a percarbonate stripper does comparatively little harm, it raises grain far less, and the colour shift is mild. For a first-time deck refinisher working alone on a warm day, that margin for error is worth something real.
          </p>

          <p>
            They are also the right answer where runoff is a problem. On a raised deck above planting beds, near a watercourse, or anywhere caustic runoff would be difficult to contain, percarbonate is a far easier product to work with responsibly. It is not entirely harmless to plants but it is in a different category.
          </p>

          <p>
            Their limitation is simply stated: they do not break films. A cured solid stain or paint is not meaningfully attacked, and applying more, or leaving it longer, or scrubbing harder does not change the outcome. What you get is a slightly dulled surface with patchy pigment removal and a ghost of the old colour, which people reasonably interpret as a defective product.
          </p>

          <p>
            Powder percarbonate concentrates are also the most economical option per square foot by a wide margin, and they store indefinitely in powder form, which makes them the sensible default to have on hand for routine deck cleaning and light stripping between full refinishes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="matching" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Matching the Stripper to What Is on Your Deck
          </h2>

          <p>
            The decision is made by identifying the existing finish, and there is a simple test that settles it without any product at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look at how the finish is failing. A film finish peels, flakes and lifts at the edges, leaving bare patches with defined boundaries. A penetrating finish fades, goes grey and wears thin in traffic paths with no defined edges and nothing to pick at. That distinction tells you almost everything.
          </p>

          <p>
            Confirm with a water test. Sprinkle water on a section. If it beads, there is still a functional finish present. If it soaks straight in, the finish is spent and whatever is left is mostly pigment in the grain, which favours percarbonate. Combine that with a look at a worn edge in raking light to check for distinct colour layers, which indicate multiple film coats.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What You See</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Finish Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Use</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Peeling in sheets or flakes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Paint or solid stain</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Caustic gel</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Peeling slightly, colour opaque</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Solid stain</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Caustic</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Faded, some sheen left</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Semi-solid or recent semi-trans</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Caustic or strong percarbonate</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Faded and grey, water soaks in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Worn semi-transparent</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Percarbonate</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Grey all over, no colour</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Finish fully spent</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Percarbonate, or just clean</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Distinct colour layers at edge</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Multiple film coats</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Caustic gel, two applications</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            If the deck has a mix, which is common where one area is sheltered and another is sun-exposed, work to the hardest case. A deck with intact solid stain under the table and bare grey wood in the middle needs caustic, applied more heavily where the film survives and lightly where it does not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do a test patch before buying in quantity. Ten minutes on a hidden square foot with a small amount of product tells you whether your chosen chemistry is working and how many rounds you are facing, and it is the difference between one correct purchase and two.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wood-damage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Each One Does to the Wood
          </h2>

          <p>
            Both products change the wood, and the difference in how much is one of the main reasons to prefer the milder option where it will do the job.
          </p>

          <p>
            Caustic darkens. The reaction with tannins produces a brown or orange discolouration that is dramatic on cedar and redwood in particular, and uneven across a deck because it depends on how long each area was in contact. Left unbrightened, that colour is what you will be staining over, and a semi-transparent finish will show it clearly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Caustic also degrades lignin, the natural adhesive between wood fibres. In moderation that is fine and is part of how the finish releases. Overdone, it leaves the surface fibres loose, which shows up as fuzziness after drying and requires sanding to correct. Softwoods, particularly cedar, are far more susceptible than dense hardwoods.
          </p>

          <p>
            Percarbonate is much gentler on both counts. It lightens rather than darkens, because the peroxide it releases bleaches grey weathered fibre, and it has comparatively little effect on lignin. A deck stripped with percarbonate usually needs no sanding at all before finishing, which is a genuine saving in time and abrasive.
          </p>

          <p>
            Neither product should be left to dry on the deck. A dried application stops working and leaves the partly dissolved finish set back into the grain harder than it was, which is true of both chemistries and is the most common technique error regardless of which bottle you bought.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Effect on Cedar Decking</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Marked</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Caustic darkening</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Slight</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Percarbonate colour change</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Often</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Sanding needed after caustic</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Rarely</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Sanding needed after percarbonate</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="brightener" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Why Caustic Makes the Brightener Mandatory
          </h2>

          <p>
            This step is presented in most instructions as a third optional product, and after a caustic strip it is not optional at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            A brightener is an acid, usually oxalic or citric, and it does two jobs. It neutralises the strongly alkaline surface a caustic stripper leaves behind, and it reverses the tannin darkening, returning the wood close to its natural colour. Both matter for what comes next.
          </p>

          <p>
            The neutralising matters because alkaline wood accepts stain unevenly, and because residual alkalinity can react with an oil-based finish over time. A deck stained directly over an unneutralised caustic strip can look acceptable on the day and develop blotching or premature failure across the following season.
          </p>

          <p>
            The colour reversal matters because the darkening is not uniform and does not weather out. Under a solid stain you would never know. Under a semi-transparent finish, which is what most people who have gone to the trouble of stripping want, the mottling shows through clearly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Note that a brightener is not the same as a deck cleaner, and the two sit adjacent on the shelf with similar promises. A cleaner is typically alkaline and removes dirt and mildew; a brightener is acidic and neutralises. Using a cleaner as the final step after a caustic strip leaves the wood alkaline, which is the opposite of the intent. Our guide on deck brightener versus deck cleaner covers the distinction.
          </p>

          <p>
            After a percarbonate strip, a brightener is worth doing but less critical, since percarbonate is mildly alkaline and does not cause tannin darkening. Treat it as a finishing step that evens the colour rather than a chemical necessity.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Safety, Plants and Runoff
          </h2>

          <p>
            The handling difference between the two is large enough to influence the choice on some sites regardless of which would strip better.
          </p>

          <p>
            Caustic strippers cause chemical burns on contact, and the exposure risk is highest in exactly the posture the job demands: kneeling, scraping, with hands close to a treated surface. Chemical-resistant gauntlet gloves that cover the wrist, sealed eye protection, long sleeves and closed shoes are the minimum. Keep a hose running nearby for immediate flushing rather than relying on a bucket.
          </p>

          <p>
            Caustic runoff kills plants outright, etches aluminium and glass, and stains concrete, pavers and stone. On a raised deck, decide where the runoff will land before the first application, not during it. Cover shrubs with plastic sheeting rather than merely wetting them, and wet and cover any hard surfaces below.
          </p>

          <p>
            Percarbonate is substantially safer on all counts. It is still alkaline and still irritating to skin and eyes, so gloves and eye protection remain sensible, but it will not burn you on brief contact and it is far more forgiving around planting. It breaks down into washing soda, water and oxygen, which is why it is the product of choice where runoff cannot be fully controlled.
          </p>

          <p>
            Both benefit from the same site practice: sheet the plants, wet down hard surfaces before you start so chemical does not soak in, and rinse thoroughly and promptly. Check local guidance on where rinse water may be discharged, particularly with caustic products and particularly near a watercourse.
          </p>

          <p>
            One last point that applies to either. If the deck or house predates 1978, test for lead before disturbing any coating. Neither chemistry is the relevant question at that point, because pressure washing lead paint spreads it across the garden and the correct handling is entirely different.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Percarbonate Stripper for a Film Finish</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Oxygen strippers oxidise pigment sitting in and on the wood; they do not break the binder in a cured acrylic film. On solid stain or paint they produce a dulled surface and a ghost of the old colour no matter how long you dwell or how hard you scrub. Film finishes need caustic.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Brightener After a Caustic Strip</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Caustic leaves the wood strongly alkaline and unevenly darkened. Alkaline wood takes stain patchily and can react with an oil finish over the following season, and the tannin darkening does not weather out. An oxalic or citric acid brightener is part of the chemistry, not a cosmetic extra.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Deck Cleaner as the Neutralising Step</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cleaners and brighteners sit together on the shelf and make similar promises, but a cleaner is typically alkaline and a brightener is acidic. Finishing a caustic strip with a cleaner leaves the wood alkaline, which is precisely what the step exists to correct.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Thin Caustic Liquid on a Painted Deck</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Paint needs 20 to 45 minutes of dwell, and a thin liquid on horizontal decking in summer can dry in ten. The application is then wasted and the softened film sets back into the grain harder than before. For paint and heavy solid stain, buy caustic in a gel or thickened form.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Over-Dwelling Caustic on Cedar</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sodium hydroxide degrades lignin, the natural binder between wood fibres, and softwoods are far more susceptible than hardwoods. Left well past its window it leaves a furred, fuzzy surface that must be sanded before finishing. Watch for the finish wrinkling or lifting rather than working to the clock.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between caustic and oxygen deck strippers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Caustic strippers use sodium hydroxide to break down the binder holding a finish together, so they strip films like solid stain and paint. Oxygen strippers use sodium percarbonate to oxidise and lift pigment sitting in the wood, so they handle penetrating stains well and barely touch films.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will an oxygen stripper remove solid stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not. Solid stain is a pigmented acrylic that forms a genuine film, and percarbonate does not attack the binder holding that film together. You will get a dulled surface with patchy pigment removal and a ghost of the old colour. Solid stain generally needs a caustic product, ideally as a gel.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does caustic stripper damage wood?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It changes it. Sodium hydroxide reacts with tannins and darkens the wood markedly, especially cedar and redwood, and it degrades lignin, so an over-dwelt application leaves a fuzzy surface needing sanding. Used within its window and followed by an acid brightener, the darkening reverses and the damage is minor.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which deck stripper is safe for plants?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sodium percarbonate is far more forgiving; it breaks down into washing soda, water and oxygen. It is not entirely harmless but it is in a different category from caustic, which kills plants on contact, etches aluminium and glass, and stains concrete and pavers. On a raised deck over planting, percarbonate is much easier to use responsibly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I tell which stripper my deck needs?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Look at how the old finish is failing. Peeling and flaking in sheets means a film finish and you need caustic. Fading, greying and wearing thin in traffic paths with no edges to pick at means a penetrating finish and percarbonate will handle it gently. A water test helps: if water soaks straight in, the finish is largely spent.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a brightener after using an oxygen stripper?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is worth doing but far less critical than after caustic. Percarbonate is only mildly alkaline and does not cause tannin darkening, so the brightener acts as a colour-evening finishing step rather than a chemical necessity. After a caustic strip it is mandatory.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I mix or combine the two types?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Do not mix them in a container. You can, however, use them in sequence on a deck with mixed conditions: caustic on the areas where a film survives, then a percarbonate clean over the whole deck to even out the grey and the traffic paths, followed by an acid brightener as the final step.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

