        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Identify the finish before you identify the product. A finish that peels and flakes is a film, and films need sodium hydroxide, ideally as a gel so it stays wet through a long dwell. A finish that fades, greys and wears thin is penetrating, and sodium percarbonate will lift it more gently, more cheaply and with far less cleanup.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If you go caustic, treat the acid brightener as part of the job rather than an optional third product. It neutralises a strongly alkaline surface that would otherwise take stain unevenly, and it reverses the tannin darkening that shows straight through any semi-transparent finish. Skipping it is the most common way a technically successful strip still produces a disappointing deck.
          </p>
        </div>

